WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.170
Do we all learn languages the same way?

00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:06.320
The quick answer is yes, but I can
refine that answer a little bit.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:08.940
Our brains learn the same way.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.870
We all have more or less similarly
structured brains, just like our digestive

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:17.430
system works the same in every person.

00:00:17.459 --> 00:00:22.700
Now, it may be that we like certain foods
and we don't like other foods, or we live

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:25.009
in an area where there's lots of fish.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:28.649
And so we tend to consume
things that are available to us,

00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:32.865
or things that we like to consume. In
our stomachs, in our digestive system,

00:00:33.055 --> 00:00:34.675
the same is true in our brains.

00:00:34.685 --> 00:00:37.885
Some people like reading and
some people don't like reading.

00:00:38.004 --> 00:00:39.515
Some people can't read.

00:00:39.634 --> 00:00:41.825
Some people have problems with their eyes.

00:00:41.835 --> 00:00:43.214
Some people are dyslexic.

00:00:43.235 --> 00:00:48.055
Some people are visually
impaired or auditorily impaired.

00:00:48.075 --> 00:00:52.585
So obviously they are going to
absorb the language in whatever

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:55.265
way suits their capability.

00:00:55.275 --> 00:00:57.935
But if we have people who are equally

00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:02.320
able to see, equally able to hear,
then essentially they're going to

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.880
process language input in the same way.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.859
Some people may prefer
speaking to listening

00:01:09.225 --> 00:01:12.565
or reading, but that doesn't
mean they learn by speaking.

00:01:12.634 --> 00:01:16.905
The only way we can learn a new
language, that is, a language that we

00:01:16.905 --> 00:01:23.345
don't have in ourselves, is to get the
language from outside ourselves. Input!

00:01:23.385 --> 00:01:25.645
And that means listening,

00:01:25.654 --> 00:01:30.835
if we are able to hear. Reading, if we
are able to see and read. A combination

00:01:31.035 --> 00:01:35.885
of both, which is ideal, because the
more different ways we can acquire

00:01:36.155 --> 00:01:39.285
the same information, the better
we're going to learn that information.

00:01:39.285 --> 00:01:41.285
But the process is the same.

00:01:41.505 --> 00:01:45.195
Now, if you Google, "Does everyone
learn the same way?" you'll find

00:01:45.195 --> 00:01:49.675
a bunch of articles, scholarly
articles from educators saying

00:01:49.675 --> 00:01:51.385
that we all learn differently.

00:01:51.455 --> 00:01:56.380
We have visual learners and auditory
learners and Kinetic learners. But

00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:00.650
in fact, there is no scientific
research which supports this.

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:04.620
In fact, when they ask people what
kind of learner they are and people

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.510
say, "I'm a visual learner" and "I'm
an auditory learner," and so forth,

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:10.950
and then they put them in separate
groups and have them learn something,

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:14.960
there's no difference whether that
person learns in the way they think

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:18.900
they are more inclined to learn; the
way they think their brain works.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:21.680
And someone who is learning in
a way, which in theory, they

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:22.880
say, "I'm not a visual learner,"

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:23.579
tt's all the same.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:26.950
So that's just a myth. And I'm going
to leave a link to some articles

00:02:27.435 --> 00:02:29.445
on the subject. But it
becomes a bit of a crutch.

00:02:29.445 --> 00:02:31.995
So, "I only can learn things if I hear them."

00:02:31.995 --> 00:02:35.835
In fact, we all learn better
if we both read and listen, when

00:02:35.835 --> 00:02:37.365
it comes to languages. Speaking,

00:02:37.365 --> 00:02:41.205
of course, in order to speak well,
we need to speak a lot. And part of

00:02:41.205 --> 00:02:45.165
speaking is that we then generate
input that's relevant to us because

00:02:45.165 --> 00:02:47.625
it's from the person we're speaking
to and you want to learn that.

00:02:48.155 --> 00:02:52.215
It's an important part of the input
process is speaking with people.

00:02:52.435 --> 00:02:56.595
And of course, an important part
of practicing speaking is speaking.

00:02:56.924 --> 00:02:59.234
And in my previous videos, I explained

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:04.930
the steps that the brain goes through in
drawing on its memory of the language and

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:09.520
its experience with the language in order
to generate an utterance in the language.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:13.199
Language learning starts with
getting the language in you,

00:03:13.369 --> 00:03:15.149
and we all do it the same way.

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:17.579
But there are two other things
that should be considered when

00:03:17.579 --> 00:03:18.950
we say do we learn the same way.

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:21.049
One is our preferences.

00:03:21.089 --> 00:03:23.399
So some people simply don't like to read.

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:28.569
So if a person doesn't like to read,
because our motivation is so important in

00:03:28.569 --> 00:03:33.140
language learning, then to push against
something that person doesn't like to

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:35.250
do is going to be counterproductive.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:39.619
So in that sense, it's a good thing
to allow people to learn in the

00:03:39.619 --> 00:03:42.840
way they find the most pleasant
because then they're most likely

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:44.819
to continue and put the effort in.

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:47.019
However, it shouldn't be used as a crutch.

00:03:47.069 --> 00:03:48.570
"I can't learn by reading."

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:49.410
No, that's not true.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:53.295
If you develop the habit of reading,
you will learn through reading. And

00:03:53.295 --> 00:03:56.595
that will reinforce the learning
you're doing through listening.

00:03:56.665 --> 00:03:59.914
Another consideration is, sort of,
the level we are in the language.

00:04:00.385 --> 00:04:03.945
Obviously at the very beginning, it's
very difficult to start speaking.

00:04:03.945 --> 00:04:05.155
We have very little to say.

00:04:05.425 --> 00:04:09.365
And if we rely on our ability to
speak, we simply go around and around

00:04:09.375 --> 00:04:10.985
using the same words all the time.

00:04:11.045 --> 00:04:15.175
So at the beginning stage, it's more
important to do a lot of listening

00:04:15.175 --> 00:04:18.685
and reading in order to build
up some experience, some memory,

00:04:18.685 --> 00:04:22.735
some knowledge of the language,
if we're at an intermediate stage.

00:04:23.125 --> 00:04:27.585
So now we're in this particular language,
we want to speak, we want to speak well.

00:04:27.785 --> 00:04:32.225
So we have accumulated now some
resources, some words, some phrases,

00:04:32.225 --> 00:04:33.665
some experience with the language.

00:04:33.835 --> 00:04:35.175
We want to get out and use it.

00:04:35.365 --> 00:04:37.355
So therefore, we need to speak more.

00:04:37.474 --> 00:04:38.404
So at that stage,

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:41.640
the speaking activity should
become more important.

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:47.360
So at different stages in our journey in
a particular language, certain activities

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:52.309
may be more important, may be preferred. 
An activity which is preferred is probably

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:55.889
going to be better because the student—
the learner—is keen to do these things.

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:59.669
But it's important to not get carried
away with certain educational fads.

00:04:59.935 --> 00:05:04.015
Like the idea that we have different
learning styles. And this myth of the

00:05:04.015 --> 00:05:09.835
learning styles, it's still strongly held.
And it seems kind of intuitive. But any

00:05:09.835 --> 00:05:14.805
scientific research on the subject has
shown that in fact, it's simply a myth.

00:05:14.855 --> 00:05:18.315
So obviously if we have a strong
preference for a certain learning

00:05:18.315 --> 00:05:19.915
activity, that's what we're going to do.

00:05:19.995 --> 00:05:24.544
And as long as we are motivated and
we spend the time with the language,

00:05:24.859 --> 00:05:28.590
we are going to learn. But we shouldn't
fool ourselves into thinking that some

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:31.689
people will only learn if they hear
the language and others will only learn

00:05:31.729 --> 00:05:35.119
if they read the language or they will
only learn if they speak the language.

00:05:35.159 --> 00:05:37.849
In fact, all of our brains
function more or less the same way.

00:05:37.879 --> 00:05:40.970
And it's a subject of great interest to
me because I'm doing all this reading on

00:05:40.989 --> 00:05:44.679
how the brain learns different things. 
But we all, basically, just as we have

00:05:44.689 --> 00:05:49.489
essentially the same digestive system,
we have the same cerebral system.

00:05:49.559 --> 00:05:52.159
So yeah, the quick answer is we all learn

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:55.210
the same way, with a few
caveats that I mentioned.

